WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Cedar’s team uses the queue correctly for a week.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:07.707
Then a supervisor starts accepting email handoffs again because it feels faster.

00:00:07.947 --> 00:00:12.725
Coordinators follow the supervisor’s example, even though they remember the new steps.

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:15.847
Reinforcement concerns sustaining change.

00:00:16.087 --> 00:00:25.346
An original Cedar response is to ask why the shortcut returned, fix any genuine friction, agree on the handoff standard and recognize accurate work.

00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:30.599
Do not reward raw ticket counts if that encourages rushed or incomplete handoffs.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:33.060
Measure the behavior you intended.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.551
For this practice case, inspect a small agreed sample of handoffs for an owner and usable next action.

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:42.927
Pair the result with a short conversation about obstacles.

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:48.180
Training-completion rate measures attendance or completion, not the quality of handoffs.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:57.531
Original follow-through plan: the shift lead checks five agreed sample handoffs on Friday, notes whether both required fields are useful, asks the

00:00:57.531 --> 00:01:02.011
team what slowed them down and reviews the next step the following Tuesday.

00:01:02.251 --> 00:01:06.582
The sample size and dates are practice choices, not validated thresholds.

00:01:06.822 --> 00:01:11.899
Avoid publishing individual rankings or collecting unnecessary personal details.

00:01:12.589 --> 00:01:17.389
A drop in use can also signal a new workload, tool or motivation barrier.

00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:21.640
Ask again rather than assuming the original problem is solved forever.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.418
Now it's your turn.

00:01:23.658 --> 00:01:27.583
Correct queue use falls after supervisors accept email again.

00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:29.210
Which plan is strongest?

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.565
